<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Two URLs, and that is the whole indexable host: the EN root and its LT
     twin. Everything else this site serves is either noindex or not a page,
     and the reasons are at the bottom of this file.

     lastmod is edited by hand when a page changes — a date that moves on its
     own says nothing. Both entries carry 2026-09-06, the day the Venues block
     replaced the markets strip in index.html and lt/index.html. Work that
     touches only assets/trust/, which neither page references, is not a change
     to either page and does not earn a date. -->
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9"
        xmlns:xhtml="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
  <url>
    <loc>https://blynai.eu/</loc>
    <lastmod>2026-09-06</lastmod>
    <xhtml:link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://blynai.eu/"/>
    <xhtml:link rel="alternate" hreflang="lt" href="https://blynai.eu/lt/"/>
    <xhtml:link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://blynai.eu/"/>
    <changefreq>monthly</changefreq><priority>1.0</priority>
  </url>
  <url>
    <loc>https://blynai.eu/lt/</loc>
    <lastmod>2026-09-06</lastmod>
    <xhtml:link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://blynai.eu/"/>
    <xhtml:link rel="alternate" hreflang="lt" href="https://blynai.eu/lt/"/>
    <xhtml:link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://blynai.eu/"/>
    <changefreq>monthly</changefreq><priority>0.9</priority>
  </url>
  <!-- The alternates above are the same three links each page carries in its
       own <head>, pointing the same way in both directions, with x-default on
       the EN root because EN is what / serves. There is no third language and
       no language-guessing redirect on / — see _redirects, which explains why
       that stays a trap we do not walk into.

       WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT LISTED

       /aktas/ was listed here, described as indexable. It is not, and it never
       was. The page is a client-rendered bundle: its served HTML carries no
       robots meta, but the template the runtime swaps into the document does,
       so the rendered DOM says <meta name="robots" content="noindex">.
       Checked against the live page in a browser, not inferred from the file.
       A sitemap entry for a noindex URL asks the crawler to index something
       the page then refuses, which Search Console reports as an error against
       the sitemap rather than a note against the page. It comes out under the
       rule this file already stated for /dokumentacija/ — noindex pages are
       not listed — rather than a new rule written to fit. Removing the
       noindex from the page instead was rejected: it is baked into the
       bundle's own template, so that edit belongs where the bundle is
       generated, not in a hand-maintained sitemap. List /aktas/ again the day
       the rendered page stops saying noindex, with the alternates omitted —
       it is Lithuanian only.

       /dokumentacija/ stays out for the reason it always did: noindex, and
       linked from nowhere. /og/og-card.html, /og/og-card-lt.html and
       /assets/trust/logos-preview.html are unlinked render sources for a
       social image and a contrast audit — nothing links them, and they are
       not pages anyone should land on.

       https://cryptosmithx.blynai.eu/ is gone from this file. It was here as a
       Google cross-submission: permitted only because both properties are
       verified in Search Console, and a flat violation of the sitemaps.org
       rule that every URL in one sitemap share one host. The thing that made
       that exception worth taking has since been built. That host now serves
       its own sitemap.xml at its root and names it from a Sitemap line in its
       own robots.txt — exactly what the previous version of this comment
       recommended as the better answer. Keeping the entry anyway, as belt and
       braces, was rejected on two counts: it duplicates a single URL that is
       already linked from both pages above and already carried by that host's
       own sitemap, and its lastmod would be maintained in this repo while the
       page ships from another one, so the only thing it could do over time is
       go stale and contradict the sitemap that is actually right. -->
</urlset>
